# Quality Gates

Before completing research, design, implementation, or review work, confirm:

- Key conclusions are supported by evidence.
- Key assumptions are labeled.
- Failure paths and boundary conditions were considered.
- Unconfirmed points are listed separately.
- Verification actions and results are recorded.

## Skill-Specific Gates

| Work type | Minimum quality gate |
|---|---|
| `$rd-requirements-analysis` | Core scenarios covered, requirements verifiable, priorities labeled, exclusions explicit |
| `$rd-doc-review` requirements mode | Completeness, verifiability, consistency, priority, and boundary checks completed |
| `$rd-technical-writing` | At least two candidate solutions when relevant, recommendation rationale clear, risks and rollback addressed |
| `$rd-doc-review` proposal mode | Requirements coverage, architecture soundness, deployment design, cost, and risk checked |
| `$rd-detailed-design` | Interfaces, data models, critical flows, failure paths, and concurrency needs specified |
| `$rd-doc-review` design mode | Interface completeness, data model, concurrency, security, and implementability checked |
| `$rd-implement` | Code aligns with design, tests or verification exist, no hardcoded secrets |
| `$rd-code-review` | Findings prioritized by severity with file and line references |
| `$rd-testing` | Normal, abnormal, and boundary cases covered; failures analyzed |
| `$rd-deployment` | Repeatable runbook, rollback plan, and post-deployment verification exist |

## Completion Report

Do not say "done" without verification. Final reports should distinguish:

- Completed changes.
- Verification run.
- Verification not run and why.
- Remaining risks.
- Follow-up recommendations when useful.

## Verification Preference

Before finishing code work, prefer configured project typecheck, lint, test, and build commands. If the project has no configured verification command, say so explicitly instead of substituting a guess.
